ange  but you would kind of envision sometimes  your boss sitting down in the stands  with a small bag of cash in his hand and  if you studied hard and got the names  and numbers right at the end of a series  he would hand you two back I guess that  was one of the motivators and also the  other side of it was you you really took  pride in wanting to do the job right  and that was part of doing it right and  you found that Sports Center I mean  people know if you're if you're  pronouncing the names right  the real confusing part was when usually  it happens with one of the guys from  Europe they would change the  pronunciation or they would say they  didn't care and then one of the PR  departments would say he's saying it  differently now and we have had that  recently Connor sherry with Pittsburgh  you know as said that his uncle like the  way that he said his name more recently  than when he first came into the league  so it's different now and there was a  guy named Teemu Selanne who came into  the league in his rookie year changed it  and it was a lot easier for people in  Winnipeg to say Salani and so he agreed  and so the Finnish way of saying it  changed to the way that all of us  anglicized it wouldn't you if your  Miroslav shirt and just said Satan right  fun with that name I remember that I  remember watching some of the Devils  like he had to play for the Devils right  I mean he's sure yeah it made a lot of  sense if he would I remember four years  ago when I was visiting you guys and one  thing that I learned you know you always  have the roundtable on what did you  learn today yes the one thing I learned  that day is that Seton likes to go glove  side low on McLovin of course remember  that sequence what a tremendous move he  made that day it was I don't know if  you've had a hockey showdown I know you  shoot POW shots a lot and you do a lot  of outside  that shooting and so forth there that  was that was noteworthy that day and I  don't know if he's ever put on the gear  sense but McLovin has something to show  because usually I go peanut butter shelf  on McLovin he's doc Emmerich he's got  Game seven coming up tonight 7:30  Eastern it'll be the Leafs in the Bruins  do you want to do like a loud building  or a quiet building to do your job  Oh loud loud and raucous and you know  we've got we've got an anthem singer  that has said that he's in his  retirement year and so this potentially  could be his last for an a-rank already  started in the mid seventies here and he  does the fist pumps after the anthem the  building is is just wacky here in Boston  and that it's a it's a genuine wackiness  there's nothing manufactured about it  they care about their Bruins and so  it'll be raucous and and the teams will  either come out nervous and tentative at  the very beginning because that does  happen in game sevens or it may just it  may just be nothing but listening to the  boards and the glass in the first five  minutes because there may be lots of  pounding it'll be fun whatever it is do  you get recognized by your voice like if  you're at a restaurant or calling down  for room service or the front desk or  anything today sometimes yes sometimes  it is flattering and it's it's nice when  people recognize you like that and  occasionally I get stopped you know I'll  miss it when it doesn't happen anymore I  remember that time when there was  someone on Johnny Carson's old show and  he said that anybody that's involved in  in the public eye there is that for  example he used John Wayne as an example  who is John Wayne get me John Wayne get  me a John Wayne type who is John Wayne  and it happens with everybody in the  public eye and when they say who is Mike  Emmerich I'll miss that whole time  because I do enjoy it when people come  up and want to take a picture or they  want me to sign something that's the fun  part of doing this too but to me the fun  part is preparing and  getting in free and getting a good seat  for t